+# Reuse: the engine, and the diff that is empty
+
+Every track before this one was forbidden to depend on another track's crate. The rule (plan §3 D6)
+has a reason: each track is a deliverable somebody can skip, and a `path` dependency on a sibling
+project turns "skip a track" into "the next one does not build".
+
+T5 is the one exception, and it is the reason the rule exists at all:
+
+> if the engine `jobq` built is really a library, then a *different* project can depend on it without
+> touching it.
+
+## The dependency
+
+```toml
+jobq = { path = "../jobq" }
+```
+
+A `path` dependency on the learner's own T3 project — the tree they wrote, not a copy of it. The
+course lays `jobq`'s reference tree as a prerequisite (`kiln` refuses to judge a meshkv step while
+`app/jobq` is missing), so the dependency is on a project that is *there*.
+
+## The acceptance is the diff
+
+"It compiles" is not the graduation. The graduate claim is that `jobq` needed **no change**: no `pub`
+widened, no `derive` added, no `cfg` flipped. A library that needed any of those was not one, and the
+step would have found out — which is the value of making the dependency the deliverable rather than a
+convenience.
+
+The step's page cannot check a diff by itself; the check is the one an operator runs:
+
+```sh
+git -C app/jobq diff --stat      # empty: nothing in T3 changed to make T5 work
+```
+
+The course's own version of the same check is that `meshkv`'s patches never touch `app/jobq/**`:
+every scaffold and solution in this track is written against `app/meshkv`, and `kiln` refuses a
+course whose patches name another track's project.
+
+## What meshkv does with it
+
+Deliberately little. The engine's `JobId` — the identity `jobq/3.1` made a type so a queue can talk
+about a job without a string — becomes a key in this store:
+
+```text
+job:<id>  →  the job's state, in whatever the engine's own serialisation is
+```
+
+That is the distributed scheduler the track's title promised: a job's state can be replicated by the
+consensus log the previous steps built, and the engine that owns the job's *behaviour* did not have to
+know. The adapter is one function; the boundary `jobq/3.1` drew is what makes it that small.
